---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import ChangelogEarly from '../components/ChangelogEarly.astro';
---
<Base title="Changelog" description="What changed in each ScadaBench release.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Changelog" title="What's new." />
  <Prose>
    <h2>0.1.37 · Commands to real devices: every type, step by step</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New: Monitor real device sends every command type in the device's file: on/off, open/close, whole numbers, lists (for example the mode), set points and raise/lower (tap changers). Each one uses the device's own control model.</li>
      <li>New: select before operate can be done step by step. Select reserves the switch and shows the time left, then Operate or Cancel. Turn on "Show Select before Operate step by step" in Command options; off, one press selects and operates as before.</li>
      <li>New: the Commands window has a search (by reference or type, for example "CSWI3" or "DPC") and the Command options: test command, interlock check, synchro check and who sends the command.</li>
      <li>New: a command can be sent straight from the Data points list: its Oper.ctlVal row shows the same buttons while the device is live and Allow commands is on.</li>
      <li>Improved: when a device refuses a command, ScadaBench says why in plain words, for example that the device's own interlocking does not allow it, that it is in Local, in Test mode or switched off, followed by the device's own answer.</li>
      <li>Improved: when the device is in Test mode, the test command option starts ticked, so its commands are accepted.</li>
      <li>Fixed: sending a command from the Commands window no longer jumps back to the top of the list; the question and the answer appear in the command's own row.</li>
      <li>Fixed: commands to a device monitored through a chosen network card also use that card for select, operate and cancel.</li>
      <li>New: Re-import CID (Devices › Files) takes a newer file of the same IED without deleting it. The IED keeps its settings and every link to the diagram, panels, sources and scenarios that the new file still has; before anything changes you see what is new, what is gone, and exactly which links cannot stay. A backup is saved first and Undo takes it back.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.36 · Big stations in one project, your addresses kept</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New: a project holds up to 500 IEDs (was 20) and up to 1 GB (was 100 MB), so a whole 400/132 kV station fits in one project.</li>
      <li>New: the window's title bar, the taskbar and Alt+Tab show the open project's name, so two stations are easy to tell apart.</li>
      <li>Fixed: an address you typed is kept. Each IED has a simulation address and a monitoring address; switching between Simulate and Monitor real device never replaces them with the address in the CID file, and an empty one starts from the other.</li>
      <li>Fixed: choosing a network card now proposes the address you saved for each IED, marked "saved address", instead of the one in the SCD.</li>
      <li>Changed: a simulated IED and a monitored real device may use the same address. Two simulated IEDs, or two monitored devices, still cannot share one.</li>
      <li>New: Monitor real device has a Network card choice per IED. ScadaBench reads the relay, and sends allowed commands, through the card you pick; "Windows chooses" stays the default.</li>
      <li>New: each SNMP device has its own Run / Stop button; the other devices keep running.</li>
      <li>New: the Station gateway and the SCADA master can run at the same time. Protocol converter still runs on its own.</li>
      <li>Fixed: Fit view on the single-line diagram now zooms out far enough to show every symbol of a large station, instead of only centring it.</li>
      <li>Fixed: in the diagram's point panel, Set sends the value you typed instead of 0.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.35 · A more dependable network, faster screens, safer saving</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Fixed: when Windows refuses an IED's network address, Start now stops and names each IED, its address and Windows' reason, instead of carrying on. Stop names any address it could not take off.</li>
      <li>Fixed: two copies of ScadaBench on one PC (for example the installed app and the portable one) no longer take each other's addresses off the ScadaBench Lab card. Each copy removes only the addresses it added.</li>
      <li>Fixed: starting and stopping IEDs, SNMP devices and the gateway at the same time can no longer leave one of them without its address.</li>
      <li>Changed: the gateway's and SCADA master's own address is added when they start or connect and taken off when they stop or disconnect.</li>
      <li>New: a running gateway shows "Address gone" within a few seconds if its listen address disappears from the PC.</li>
      <li>Fixed: on a network card with no cable plugged in, ScadaBench now says the card is disconnected instead of adding an address that cannot work (after Repair updates the network service).</li>
      <li>Fixed: Stop of a Modbus gateway with a SCADA master connected but idle took up to 16 seconds and could fail; it now stops at once. When the gateway cannot listen, the message says why (port in use, address not on this PC, or blocked by Windows).</li>
      <li>Fixed: a real IED that stops answering while its connection stays open is now marked stale and reconnected, instead of showing frozen values as live.</li>
      <li>Faster: Start is quicker because ScadaBench keeps the IED programs ready while stopped; Stop with a real device connected, or while it is still trying to reach one that does not answer, ends at once; the SCADA master's Connect answers as soon as the link is open and the points fill in as they arrive.</li>
      <li>Faster: setting a value, Test buttons and GOOSE sends no longer reload the whole project; moving equipment on the diagram sends far fewer requests; large gateway point lists redraw only the rows that changed; idle pages stop asking the engine for updates.</li>
      <li>Fixed: the first Undo after moving equipment on the diagram now works, and a second browser tab shows when auto save is switched off.</li>
      <li>Fixed: a change to the single-line diagram made while the project was being saved is no longer lost. If a save fails, your drawing stays on screen, the top bar shows "Not saved", and ScadaBench tries again on your next change or Save.</li>
      <li>Improved: a project opens normally after a power cut or a forced close. A damaged recovery file is moved aside, never deleted, and the last saved state opens. Save now tells you when the file could not be written.</li>
      <li>Fixed: Stop, closing ScadaBench and Clean up remove only the network addresses ScadaBench added itself. Addresses you set in Windows, and addresses on cards that get theirs automatically, are never removed.</li>
      <li>Improved: gateway Stop cancels SCADA commands that are still waiting. When a real device does not answer a command in time, ScadaBench says the result is unknown and asks you to check the position before trying again; a second command to the same point is refused while the first one runs.</li>
      <li>Fixed: Stop and closing ScadaBench no longer wait forever when one of its background programs stops responding.</li>
      <li>Improved: values read from a real IED are marked stale when reading fails, instead of showing the last value as current.</li>
      <li>Improved: the Modbus gateway frees connections that have been idle for 60 seconds, so masters can always reconnect, and refuses to start when two points share a register, naming both points.</li>
      <li>Improved: if a screen fails to draw, ScadaBench shows "Something went wrong. Your IEDs are still running." with a Reload button.</li>
      <li>New: uninstalling ScadaBench offers to remove the firewall rules it added and switch Windows' own rules back on.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.34 · Real devices stay read only until you allow commands</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Changed: an IED set to "Monitor real device" is now read only. Each real IED has its own Allow commands switch in Devices › Overview, off by default. SCADA commands through the gateway reach a real IED only when its switch and the gateway's "Allow commands to real IEDs" are both on.</li>
      <li>New: while Allow commands is on and the IED is live, Devices › Data points can send Close/Open (or On/Off) to it. Every command asks first, uses the device's own control model, and shows what the device answered and where the position went.</li>
      <li>New: after connecting, ScadaBench reads how the real device itself takes each command of the SCD (its control model, select time and Local/Remote) and shows where it differs from the file. It only reads.</li>
      <li>Improved: command screens in Devices, the gateway point list and the SCADA master open as a pop-up, so the data points stay where they are.</li>
      <li>New: File › Turn off auto save. Your changes are then kept while ScadaBench is open and written only when you choose Save project or press Ctrl+S; the top bar shows when something is not saved. Auto save is on by default and ScadaBench remembers your choice.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.33 · Nothing left on your network, addresses edited where you see them</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New: change an IED's network address right on the Devices page. Click the address under "Clients connect to", type the new one and press Enter. On the ScadaBench Lab card it is checked and used at once; on a card such as Ethernet, Windows asks once to approve it.</li>
      <li>New: SNMP devices have their own address on the network, shown and edited in each device's Settings tab, with the same checks as IEDs. Two devices can no longer share one address.</li>
      <li>New: simulated SNMP devices also run on the ScadaBench Lab card, so SCADA and network management software on this PC can poll them at their own addresses.</li>
      <li>Improved: on Ethernet and other network cards, SNMP device addresses now follow the devices like IED addresses do: they are added when the devices start and removed when they stop or ScadaBench closes.</li>
      <li>Improved: when ScadaBench opens, and after Stop, it looks for addresses an earlier version or a forced close left on your network cards. If it finds any, it lists them and removes them in one step with your approval. Addresses you set yourself are never touched.</li>
      <li>Improved: when an address on a network card is already used by another device, Start now names the IED and that device instead of asking you to apply the address plan again.</li>
      <li>Fixed: if the ScadaBench network service is missing, out of date or was turned off by another program, putting IEDs on Ethernet or another network card now sets it up within the same Windows approval, and Start turns it back on by itself (Windows asks once). No addresses are left behind after Stop.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.32 · Undo everywhere, New and Close project</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New: Undo and Redo next to the File menu work on every page: IEDs, the single-line diagram, the Test page and the gateway lists. Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y work too. The last 20 changes can be undone, and the button tip says what it takes back. Changes made while IEDs run are not undone.</li>
      <li>New: File › New project, Save as and Close project. Save as lets you pick the name and folder; New and Close offer to save a copy first.</li>
      <li>Improved: IED Explorer refreshes the data objects you open every 2 seconds by itself, with one Read all now button instead of a refresh arrow on each object.</li>
      <li>Improved: Stop and Disconnect buttons are red, and the breaker, disconnector and earth switch panels say which buttons send a real MMS command (Operate) and which only force a value in the simulated IED (Test).</li>
      <li>Improved: when an IED cannot start because its address or port is not available, the message now says why (the port is held by another program, which is named; the address is not available on this PC; or the network card is unplugged), and the reason is kept in the run log.</li>
      <li>Fixed: creating the lab network card no longer fails on PCs where Windows cannot change the card's DNS registration setting; the card is set up without it.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.31 · Faster Start and more reliable network setup</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New: on Ethernet and other network cards with a fixed address, IED addresses are approved once. After that, Start adds them and Stop removes them without a Windows prompt, and closing ScadaBench removes them as well.</li>
      <li>Faster: each simulated IED loads its data model up to five times faster, so Start takes about half as long on projects with large IEDs.</li>
      <li>Faster: opening a project and loading the first page take about half as long, and the first Start after opening is as quick as the ones that follow.</li>
      <li>Faster: placing IEDs on a real network card applies all Windows changes in one step, in seconds instead of up to two minutes. Changes that Windows completes late are still recorded, so Clean up finds every one.</li>
      <li>Improved: moving IEDs to the ScadaBench Lab card first removes the addresses ScadaBench had placed on the previous card.</li>
      <li>Improved: the interface works entirely offline. Fonts, logos and every part of the page come from ScadaBench itself, and the page does not load anything from the internet.</li>
      <li>Fixed: some antivirus products could block the lab network service during installation. The service now runs as its own dedicated program.</li>
      <li>Fixed: ScadaBench no longer places fixed addresses on a network card that gets its address automatically (DHCP), so that card keeps its connection. The network page explains why such a card cannot be used.</li>
      <li>Fixed: Repair now updates a lab network service that is already running.</li>
      <li>Upgrading: if you already use the lab network, press Repair once on the lab network page (one Windows prompt) to install the new service.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.30 · Faster Start and Stop, lighter on Windows</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Faster: Start is about twice as fast. ScadaBench now reads the first values of all your IEDs at the same time instead of one point after another, and works out each IED's test modes once instead of on every change.</li>
      <li>Faster: on the ScadaBench Lab network, Start puts all IED addresses on the card in one step and Stop takes them off in one step, instead of about a second per IED.</li>
      <li>Lighter on Windows: ScadaBench no longer asks the Windows DNS Client about every network card when it looks at your cards, and the ScadaBench Lab card no longer registers its addresses in DNS. On PCs with many network cards (VMware, Hyper-V, VPNs) this kept the "DNS Client" service busy.</li>
      <li>Already use the lab network? Press Repair once on the lab network page (one Windows prompt) to get the faster lab service. ScadaBench shows a line with the button until then.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.29 · Lab addresses only while your IEDs run</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Changed: on the ScadaBench Lab network, an IED's address is now on your PC only while that IED runs. "Use the lab network" saves the addresses; Start puts them on, and Stop takes them off again. Before, they stayed on the PC until Windows restarted, even if you never pressed Start.</li>
      <li>Closing ScadaBench also takes the addresses off. If ScadaBench was closed by force, it cleans up the leftover addresses the next time it opens.</li>
      <li>Ping an IED after you press Start. Changing an address while running still moves the IED at once.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.28 · Lab network on this PC, GOOSE follows your IEDs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New: a lab network on this PC. Each simulated IED answers at its own address from the SCL file, for example 192.168.216.1, without a virtual machine. Ping, MMS clients such as IEDScout or a SCADA, and GOOSE work on those addresses, and nothing goes out on your Ethernet or Wi-Fi.</li>
      <li>Windows asks for permission once, to add the "ScadaBench Lab" card (the Windows loopback adapter, no download, no restart). After that, Use and every address change need no prompt. Type another address and it moves at once, even while running; the SCL file is never changed.</li>
      <li>Clash checks: an address used twice or one of your PC's own addresses is refused; when a real device on your Wi-Fi or Ethernet already answers at an address, ScadaBench warns you and offers a free one.</li>
      <li>New: GOOSE now follows your IEDs. It goes to the same network as your simulated IEDs (the lab network, a real card, or only inside ScadaBench), and sending next to a real device asks you once first. A missing Npcap is one clear line with a download link.</li>
      <li>Uninstalling ScadaBench also removes the lab card and its service.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.27 · NR/NARI relays and SCD deadbands</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New: NR and NARI relays (for example PCS-9705 and PCS-921S) now import. They have three access points; ScadaBench simulates the station bus one, S1, which a SCADA connects to, exactly as the SCD defines it. The import dialog says which access points (process-bus GOOSE on G1, Sampled Values on M1) are not simulated.</li>
      <li>New: deadbands from the SCD. Each simulated IED has a "Use SCD deadbands" switch. When it is on, a measurement is reported only when it moves more than its db, like a real IED. It is off by default, so nothing changes until you turn it on.</li>
      <li>New: the IED page lists every measurement's deadband in plain words. A measurement without a measuring range (rangeC) never gets a deadband, and the list says so.</li>
      <li>New: a SCADA can change a measurement's db or zeroDb over MMS when the SCD allows it (valKind Set or not given). Read-only values stay refused, as on the real IED.</li>
      <li>Values you type by hand always go out; only automatic sources are filtered by a deadband.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.26 · Lighter and faster with many IEDs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Faster: ScadaBench now needs about half the memory and a third of the CPU for large stations. On a 20-IED station memory dropped from about 1.6 GB to under 0.8 GB at its peak, and the project opens and starts faster.</li>
      <li>Faster: Run starts sooner (2.3 s instead of 3.2 s for 20 IEDs), reopening a project is about twice as fast, and SLD commands answer a little quicker.</li>
      <li>Memory is given back to Windows after opening a project, Run and Stop, so ScadaBench no longer keeps showing its highest memory use.</li>
      <li>Nothing else changes: the same screens, the same IED behaviour on the network, and the same reports and GOOSE messages.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.25 · IEDs on the diagram, a smart point picker, many vendor files fixed</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New: an IED symbol for the single-line diagram. GOOSE now travels the way it does in a real station: from the whole sending IED to every IED that subscribes to it. "Add all GOOSE IEDs" places them for you, and a chip names subscribers that are not on the drawing.</li>
      <li>New: send a GOOSE message from the IED symbol, from any equipment's GOOSE link (one value, or open and closed separately), or from a point panel row.</li>
      <li>New: a smarter point picker. Chips filter by point type (single point, double point, measurement, setpoint, integer, command and more) in the usual colours, with counts, a filter by logical node and search by number or description. Suggestions name the equipment you are binding.</li>
      <li>Fixed: many SCL files from ABB, Siemens, GE, SEL, Schneider, NR and others that could not be imported before now import (Unicode text, unusual names, enum defaults, trigger options, datasets without a data object name, files up to 100 MB). Import errors now say where in the file the problem is.</li>
      <li>Fixed: Beh follows Mod as the standard describes, large IEDs start reliably, quieter logs, correct timestamps at Run start and the command originator shown as "ScadaBench".</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.24 · Your drawing always in view, push buttons as point lists</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Fixed: the single-line diagram could open on an empty area when one symbol lay far away from the rest of the drawing. The view now shows your drawing, and a note names the far-away symbol with a "Bring it here" button.</li>
      <li>New: "Put push buttons into point panels" in a push button's properties turns your push buttons into one point panel per IED. Status buttons become ON/OFF rows and command buttons become command rows, with the same signals. Undo brings the buttons back.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.23 · Many fixes from a full test of the app</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>IED Explorer no longer selects a control just because you opened it. Reading a select-before-operate control could reserve it on the device; that field is now never read.</li>
      <li>A command sent with the Test option no longer moves the breaker on the diagram when the IED is in service and ignores it.</li>
      <li>Tap-changer transformer: its settings list the tap changer controls of your IEDs again, and changing the IED never removes a working setup.</li>
      <li>After Stop and Run, the diagram shows the values the IEDs really start with, not the ones from the last run.</li>
      <li>When a device refuses Cancel, the selection is kept so Operate still works, and the reason is explained.</li>
      <li>DNP3 gateway: output statuses are reported as good and command results arrive at once as events.</li>
      <li>SCADA tools: Find registers has a Stop button, a refused connection says nothing is listening, Modbus writes from the master can choose the word order, and the protocol converter has the What to read tab for Modbus devices.</li>
      <li>Activity is no longer filled with empty "report created" rows, menus close properly, Enter keeps the confirm question open, and many texts are clearer and no longer cut off at 1366×768.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.22 · Commands everywhere, point panels, tap changers, SCADA point lists</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every IEC 61850 command type: besides breakers and switches you can now send integer and enum commands, analogue set points, binary step and integer step commands (tap raise / lower), in all four control models, from IED Explorer and the single-line diagram. Tap and set point commands also work through the SCADA gateway.</li>
      <li>Select before operate is one click on the single-line diagram, like a station HMI: ScadaBench selects and operates for you. Turn on "Show Select before Operate step by step" under Command options to see each step and its countdown.</li>
      <li>New Point panel in the single-line diagram: many points of one IED in one box. Read statuses, positions, measurements, tap positions and counters, and send commands or set points from a small popup.</li>
      <li>New tap-changer transformer symbol with tap position, Raise / Lower, Auto / Manual and a simulated voltage regulator.</li>
      <li>GOOSE on the diagram is clearer: one change shows one note ("Open · 7 of 7 IEDs got it") and one line to each IED that receives it.</li>
      <li>SCADA gateway: Add points from your IEDs, from an Excel template or by typing them, every row checked first. New point types for tap position, step commands and set points on IEC 104, DNP3 and Modbus.</li>
      <li>SCADA master: the most used commands of each protocol in one click, "Repeat automatically", and your own point list: add the addresses your SCADA expects (or copy them from the gateway) and see what was received, what is missing and what has the wrong type.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>0.1.21 · Clearer VM setup, saves by itself, safer network setup</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The activation screen has a new "Using a virtual machine?" section next to the offline options, with the three steps to use your PC's license inside a VM. Both options start closed, so the screen stays short.</li>
      <li>A VM keeps looking for your licensed PC by itself and opens ScadaBench as soon as it finds it. Nothing to type in the VM.</li>
      <li>Fixed: right after you turn on "Let my virtual machines use this license", Settings no longer says the helper is not running while it is still starting.</li>
      <li>New picture guide on the tutorials page: Use ScadaBench in a virtual machine.</li>
      <li>Everything saves by itself: SNMP device settings, the SCADA gateway point list, test sequences and new gateway points no longer need a Save button. A wrong value shows a red line under the field and is not saved until you fix it.</li>
      <li>Undo in the single-line diagram now works from your very first change, and deleting a wired symbol comes back with its wires in one Undo.</li>
      <li>Network setup checks the addresses you type: an address another device already uses, or your PC's own address, is shown at once and cannot be applied. The button shows "Checking the addresses…" while it checks.</li>
      <li>Many small screen fixes at 1366×768: no more cut-off buttons, names or menus in the tools, the diagram side panel, Activity and the sidebar.</li>
    </ul>
    <ChangelogEarly />
  </Prose>
</Base>
